KW  - Tunnel boring machine
KW  - Geological forecasting
KW  - Hidden Markov random fields
KW  - Semi-supervised learning
AB  - Forecasting the geological condition ahead of the tunnel face in shield tunnelling is crucial for ensuring both construction efficiency and safety. However, most existing studies belong to supervised data-driven methods that require a complete training dataset, which is not available before a shield machine launches. This paper proposes a semi-supervised learning approach for forecasting geological profiles ahead of the shield-driven tunnel face, with the advantage of continuously integrating borehole data and shield operational parameters within a Hidden Markov Random Field (HMRF) framework. Specifically, an initial geological profile is estimated using borehole data based on the Markov Random Field method, which is subsequently updated by assimilating real-time shield operational parameters that are statistically correlated with geological profiles through a Gaussian Mixture Model. In order to achieve the multi-ring advance predictions ahead of the tunnel face, the Multi-Layer Hidden Markov Random Field (ML-HMRF) model is proposed. This enables proactive predictions of unknown geological profiles over multiple rings ahead of a shield machine. The proposed method is applied to the Zhuhai tunnel project in China, demonstrating its capability to accurately predict subsurface geological profiles several rings ahead of the tunnel face. Compared with other machine learning approaches, the proposed ML-HMRF method achieves higher accuracy in the prediction of the first five rings and performs robustly in areas with abrupt geological transitions. A sensitivity analysis further demonstrates that the model retains high accuracy even under sparse borehole conditions and achieves optimal performance at moderate neighborhood sizes.
ER  - 

