AB  - Strength development in waste-derived cementitious composites is governed by complex, multiscale physical–chemical–mechanical interactions that are insufficiently represented in conventional data-driven models. Most existing machine learning approaches rely on empirical mix-design correlations, limiting their generalizability across heterogeneous material systems and novel waste-derived constituents. This study proposes a domain-adaptation-driven transfer learning framework that systematically incorporates experimentally informed physical, chemical, and mechanical descriptors into predictive modeling under data-scarce conditions to address this limitation. A limited yet knowledge-rich dataset is integrated to enhance model interpretability and cross-domain transferability. Dimensionality reduction is performed using principal component analysis to mitigate redundancy and improve learning efficiency. The distributional divergence between the source and target domains, quantified using Maximum Mean Discrepancy, is reduced by 72–76 % following reweighting-based semi-supervised domain adaptation, leading to improved predictive accuracy. A graphical user interface is developed and validated through continuous integration workflows to facilitate practical adoption. Uncertainty quantification through confidence interval analysis demonstrates the superiority of the proposed framework in low-data regimes. The presented approach advances the applicability of interpretable, adaptive machine learning for sustainable construction materials and provides an accessible informatics platform that bridges experimental knowledge and real-world engineering decision-making.
